```java
// Spring Cloud Stream / Sleuth automatically propagate traceparent, but you can do it manually:
@Component
class HeaderTracingInterceptor implements ProducerInterceptor<Object, Object> {
    @Override public ProducerRecord<Object, Object> onSend(ProducerRecord<Object, Object> r) {
        Optional.ofNullable(MDC.get("traceId"))
            .ifPresent(t -> r.headers().add("traceparent", t.getBytes()));
        return r;
    }
    @Override public void onAcknowledgement(RecordMetadata m, Exception e) {}
    @Override public void close() {}
    @Override public void configure(Map<String, ?> configs) {}
}
```

- **Always include**: `traceparent` (W3C), `eventType`, `schemaVersion`, `sourceSystem`, optional `idempotencyKey`. Consumers can route, dedupe, and trace without deserializing the body — useful for poison messages and metrics.
- Headers are **opaque bytes** — encode strings as UTF-8 by convention. Spring Kafka's `DefaultKafkaHeaderMapper` handles the conversions automatically.
- Keep header payloads small (under a few KB total). They consume broker memory and bandwidth on every message.
- Distributed tracing libraries (Micrometer Tracing, Brave, OpenTelemetry) propagate `traceparent` automatically when you use the auto-configured `KafkaTemplate` — manual injection is the fallback for code that builds raw `ProducerRecord`s.
